# /sdd-clean

Purpose:
  Clear all SDD working context and start fresh for the next task —
  delegating the final context wipe to the AI agent's NATIVE /clear (or
  equivalent) so the agent's own memory management is never fought or
  corrupted (Phase 152 rename: was sdd-clear / auto-generated stub).

Owns: commands/sdd-clean.sdd

Description:
  Clear all context and start fresh for next task. Use when transitioning
  between distinct tasks to prevent context pollution. Delegates to the
  host agent's native clear command.

Usage:
  /sdd-clean
  /sdd-clean --save-state
  /sdd-clean --archive

Options:
  --save-state   Save current state before clearing
  --archive      Archive current context to history

Behavior:
  1. If --save-state: persist current task state (checkpoints [RT8])
  2. If --archive: move context to history log ([R57] append-only)
  3. Append the /sdd* prompt pair to prompts/history/prompt-pairs.jsonl ([R104])
  4. DELEGATE: instruct the agent to run its NATIVE /clear (Claude Code,
     Kilo /clear) — never reimplement or shadow native context commands
  5. Reset SDD working memory to minimal context (INDEX.sdd only)

Output:
  [tokens: cleared]
  [state: fresh]
  [history: archived|discarded]
  [native: /clear delegated]

Navigation:
  INDEX: @INDEX.sdd
  CompactCommand: @sdd-compact.sdd
  MemoryModel: @../runtime/memory-model.sdd

State: +
   (Phase 152: renamed sdd-clear -> sdd-clean per user naming; behavior
   now delegates the wipe to the host agent's native /clear)
